{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"# Reporting Plugin Platform — Reference Architecture

This design supports independently developed plugins without granting them direct access to the reporting tool’s process, filesystem, secrets, or database.

```text
Plugin package → Registry → Resolver/lockfile → Plugin supervisor
                                               ├─ sandboxed plugin process
                                               ├─ capability gateway
                                               ├─ lifecycle manager
                                               └─ API/RPC compatibility layer
```

## 1. Plugin model

A plugin is an immutable, signed package with:

- `plugin.json` manifest
- Bundled executable or WASM module
- Optional assets, schemas, and migrations
- Integrity hash and publisher signature

Supported extension points:

| Extension point | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `source` | Import data from approved external systems |
| `transform` | Normalize, enrich, filter, or aggregate report data |
| `renderer` | Produce HTML, PDF, CSV, XLSX, JSON, or custom output |
| `sink` | Deliver generated reports to email, storage, APIs, etc. |
| `metric` | Add computed metrics or quality checks |
| `command` | Add explicit user/admin actions |
| `ui-panel` | Add isolated configuration or report UI modules |

Plugins cannot add arbitrary server routes, database tables, background schedulers, or global UI code. Those capabilities must be expressed as future versioned extension points.

## 2. Package and manifest

Package name:

```text
@publisher/plugin-name@1.4.0
```

Archive layout:

```text
plugin.json
dist/entry.js
dist/ui.js
schemas/
assets/
LICENSE
SIGNATURE
```

### Manifest schema

Use JSON Schema draft 2020-12. The platform validates it before installation and again before activation.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://reporting.example.com/schemas/plugin-manifest/v1.json",
  "title": "Reporting Tool Plugin Manifest",
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": [
    "manifestVersion",
    "id",
    "version",
    "displayName",
    "publisher",
    "api",
    "runtime",
    "entrypoints",
    "permissions"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "manifestVersion": {
      "const": 1
    },
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$"
    },
    "version": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(-[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?(\\+[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$"
    },
    "displayName": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 100
    },
    "description": {
      "type": "string",
      "maxLength": 1000
    },
    "publisher": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["id", "name"],
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "type": "string",
          "pattern": "^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$"
        },
        "name": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 100 },
        "url": { "type": "string", "format": "uri" }
      }
    },
    "api": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["version"],
      "properties": {
        "version": {
          "type": "string",
          "pattern": "^([1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$"
        },
        "minimumHostVersion": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "runtime": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["kind"],
      "properties": {
        "kind": {
          "enum": ["node-process", "wasm-wasi"]
        },
        "nodeVersion": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "memoryMiB": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 32,
          "maximum": 1024,
          "default": 256
        },
        "cpuSecondsPerJob": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 3600,
          "default": 300
        }
      }
    },
    "entrypoints": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "required": ["id", "type", "entry"],
        "properties": {
          "id": {
            "type": "string",
            "pattern": "^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}$"
          },
          "type": {
            "enum": [
              "source",
              "transform",
              "renderer",
              "sink",
              "metric",
              "command",
              "ui-panel"
            ]
          },
          "entry": {
            "type": "string",
            "pattern": "^(dist|assets)/[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+$"
          },
          "inputSchema": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "outputSchema": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "configSchema": {
            "type": "string"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "permissions": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "network": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "additionalProperties": false,
            "required": ["host"],
            "properties": {
              "host": { "type": "string" },
              "methods": {
                "type": "array",
                "items": { "enum": ["GET", "POST", "PUT", "PATCH", "DELETE"] }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "secrets": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": { "type": "string" }
        },
        "storage": {
          "type": "object",
          "additionalProperties": false,
          "properties": {
            "read": { "type": "boolean", "default": false },
            "write": { "type": "boolean", "default": false },
            "maxMiB": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 1024 }
          }
        },
        "reportData": {
          "enum": ["none", "metadata", "read"]
        }
      },
      "required": ["reportData"]
    },
    "dependencies": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "propertyNames": {
        "pattern": "^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$"
      },
      "additionalProperties": {
        "type": "string"
      }
    },
    "configuration": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "schema": { "type": "string" },
        "version": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1 }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Example:

```json
{
  "manifestVersion": 1,
  "id": "acme-xlsx-renderer",
  "version": "2.3.0",
  "displayName": "Acme XLSX Renderer",
  "publisher": { "id": "acme", "name": "Acme Inc." },
  "api": { "version": "1.2", "minimumHostVersion": "4.8.0" },
  "runtime": { "kind": "wasm-wasi", "memoryMiB": 256 },
  "entrypoints": [{
    "id": "xlsx",
    "type": "renderer",
    "entry": "dist/renderer.wasm",
    "inputSchema": "schemas/report-model-v1.json",
    "outputSchema": "schemas/render-result-v1.json"
  }],
  "permissions": {
    "reportData": "read",
    "storage": { "read": false, "write": true, "maxMiB": 50 }
  }
}
```

## 3. Registry and installation

The registry is the source of truth for discoverable packages; the local installation store is the source of truth for what is enabled in a tenant or workspace.

Registry records must include:

- Plugin ID, version, manifest, package URL
- SHA-256 package digest
- Publisher identity and signature
- Publication time, deprecation/revocation status
- Compatibility metadata
- Security scan and certification status
- Release notes and SBOM

Installation is immutable and reproducible:

1. Resolve exact versions from requested ranges.
2. Validate manifest and dependency graph.
3. Download and verify SHA-256 digest.
4. Verify publisher signature against a trusted key.
5. Scan package and generate/store an SBOM.
6. Persist exact package versions and digests in `plugins.lock`.
7. Require tenant admin approval for new permissions.
8. Activate only after successful lifecycle initialization.

`plugins.lock` is committed or stored with workspace configuration:

```json
{
  "lockVersion": 1,
  "plugins": {
    "acme-xlsx-renderer": {
      "version": "2.3.0",
      "digest": "sha256:...",
      "publisher": "acme",
      "enabledEntrypoints": ["xlsx"]
    }
  }
}
```

Dependency policy:

- Plugins may depend only on declared shared plugin APIs, never arbitrary installed packages.
- Dependency resolution permits one version per plugin ID per workspace.
- Circular dependencies are rejected.
- A plugin’s dependency cannot gain permissions on its behalf.
- Lockfile changes require review/audit events.

## 4. Versioned API contract

Use three independently versioned contracts:

| Contract | Purpose | Compatibility policy |
|---|---|---|
| Manifest | Package metadata and install rules | `manifestVersion` only changes for breaking schema changes |
| Plugin API | Functions and models exposed to plugin authors | SemVer: major breaking, minor additive |
| RPC protocol | Host ↔ sandbox messages | Explicit protocol version negotiation |

A plugin declares `api.version: "1.2"`.

The host accepts it when:

```text
plugin API major == host API major
plugin API minor <= host supported minor
```

For example, host API `1.5` supports plugins targeting `1.0` through `1.5`. A plugin targeting `2.0` is rejected before loading.

Every RPC request includes the negotiated API version:

```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": "req_018",
  "method": "report.render",
  "params": {
    "apiVersion": "1.2",
    "executionId": "run_123",
    "input": { }
  }
}
```

All public models have named schemas and versions, such as:

```text
reporting.report-model/v1
reporting.render-result/v1
reporting.plugin-config/v1
```

Never alter an existing schema in place. Add optional fields in a minor API release; create `/v2` for semantic or structural breaks.

The SDK should expose only stable capability-scoped APIs:

```ts
interface PluginContext {
  plugin: Readonly<{ id: string; version: string }>;
  execution: Readonly<{ id: string; deadline: string; tenantId: string }>;
  logger: Logger;
  report: ReportDataCapability;
  storage?: PluginStorageCapability;
  network?: NetworkCapability;
  secrets?: SecretCapability;
  artifacts: ArtifactCapability;
  cancellation: AbortSignal;
}

interface Renderer {
  render(
    input: ReportModelV1,
    config: unknown,
    context: PluginContext
  ): Promise<RenderResultV1>;
}
```

No host internal objects, raw database clients, raw HTTP clients, environment variables, or unrestricted filesystem paths are part of the API.

## 5. Sandboxed execution boundary

Treat every plugin as untrusted code, including first-party plugins.

Preferred runtimes:

- `wasm-wasi` for renderers, transforms, and deterministic compute.
- Isolated Node subprocesses for integrations that need a richer runtime.
- Never execute plugin code in the reporting service’s process.

Each plugin execution receives:

- A fresh subprocess or WASM instance.
- Read-only package mount.
- Empty or dedicated ephemeral working directory.
- Private persistent storage namespace, if granted.
- No inherited environment variables.
- No inherited open file descriptors.
- CPU, memory, process, file-size, and wall-clock limits.
- Structured stdin/stdout RPC only.

Host-enforced controls:

| Area | Policy |
|---|---|
| Filesystem | Plugin sees package files and its capability-backed storage only. |
| Network | Denied by default; approved requests go through a host egress proxy. |
| Secrets | Returned by opaque secret handles or injected directly into an approved outbound request; never globally exposed. |
| Data | Host passes only the data model allowed by `reportData` permission. |
| Database | No direct database connectivity. |
| Subprocesses | Denied except in a separately approved runtime profile. |
| UI | Sandboxed iframe/webview with restrictive CSP and postMessage RPC. |
| Logging | Structured, rate-limited, secret-redacted host collector. |

The egress proxy should enforce domain allowlists, TLS, request size, method restrictions, per-plugin rate limits, and an audit trail. SSRF protections must deny private/link-local addresses, metadata endpoints, and DNS rebinding.

Sandbox escapes, permission violations, resource-limit breaches, and malformed protocol messages immediately terminate the execution and emit a security audit event.

## 6. Lifecycle hooks

Lifecycle is controlled by the host, never by package import side effects.

```text
installed → validated → loading → active → draining → unloaded
                       ↘ failed / quarantined
```

Plugin contract:

```ts
interface PluginModule {
  onLoad?(context: LoadContext): Promise<void>;
  onStart?(context: StartContext): Promise<void>;
  onStop?(context: StopContext): Promise<void>;
  onUnload?(context: UnloadContext): Promise<void>;

  onBeforeReload?(context: ReloadContext): Promise<PluginSnapshot | undefined>;
  onAfterReload?(
    context: ReloadContext,
    snapshot?: PluginSnapshot
  ): Promise<void>;
}
```

Rules:

- `onLoad`: Validate configuration, register declared entrypoints, allocate no long-lived external work.
- `onStart`: Begin accepting executions only after the host marks the plugin active.
- `onStop`: Stop new work and cancel or drain in-flight work by deadline.
- `onUnload`: Release resources; always best-effort.
- Hooks have fixed deadlines and are not retried automatically except transient startup policy chosen by the host.
- Hook failures are isolated to the plugin; they cannot crash the reporting service.

### Reload transaction

Reload is a two-phase operation:

1. Resolve and validate the replacement package.
2. Start and health-check the candidate in a new sandbox.
3. Ask the old instance for an optional bounded snapshot.
4. Restore snapshot into the candidate.
5. Atomically route new work to the candidate.
6. Drain the old instance.
7. Stop and unload the old instance.

If steps 2–4 fail, keep the old instance serving. If the candidate fails after cutover, revert routing when possible; otherwise disable the plugin and use the configured fallback.

Snapshots are encrypted, size-limited, versioned, and only available to the same plugin ID unless an explicit migration relationship is approved.

## 7. Execution and failure behavior

Each report execution gets an `executionId`, deadline, cancellation signal, and immutable input.

- Calls are idempotency-aware: host-generated idempotency keys are mandatory for sinks.
- Retries are host-owned, not plugin-owned.
- Source and sink retries use exponential backoff with bounded attempts.
- Renderer and transform failures fail only their report job unless marked required.
- Circuit breakers disable repeatedly failing integrations temporarily.
- A plugin is quarantined after configurable repeated crashes, protocol violations, or security events.
- Disabled, quarantined, deprecated, and revoked states are visible to administrators.

Define fallbacks per extension point, for example:

```text
primary PDF renderer fails → use built-in renderer → mark report degraded
required compliance sink fails → job remains failed; do not silently deliver partial result
optional Slack sink fails → report succeeds; delivery warning recorded
```

## 8. Configuration and secrets

Each plugin configuration has:

- JSON Schema for validation
- Explicit configuration schema version
- Per-tenant/workspace instance
- Encrypted-at-rest values
- Audit history with secret redaction
- Migration path for config updates

Configuration migrations are explicit:

```ts
interface ConfigMigrator {
  migrate(
    fromVersion: number,
    config: unknown
  ): Promise<{ version: number; config: unknown }>;
}
```

Secrets are stored by the host and referenced by identifier. Plugin configuration may contain:

```json
{
  "apiBaseUrl": "https://api.example.com",
  "credential": { "$secretRef": "tenant/acme-api-token" }
}
```

The plugin receives neither the encrypted value nor a secrets vault client. The host resolves the secret only when the capability permits the requested operation.

## 9. Governance, trust, and operations

Publisher trust tiers:

- `core`: maintained by the reporting team; bundled and automatically trusted.
- `verified`: signature verified and security-reviewed.
- `community`: signed but requires explicit tenant-admin approval.
- `blocked`: revoked or disallowed; cannot activate.

Required operational features:

- Audit trail for publish, install, enable, disable, permission approval, update, rollback, and quarantine.
- Per-plugin metrics: execution count, latency, memory, errors, retry rate, egress usage.
- Correlated structured logs using tenant, report, plugin, and execution IDs.
- Health endpoint per active plugin instance.
- Admin UI showing permissions, versions, digest, publisher, dependency tree, and current lifecycle state.
- Emergency global kill switch by plugin ID/version/digest.
- Revocation feed checked before activation and periodically during operation.

## 10. Developer experience and certification

Provide an SDK, local sandbox runner, manifest validator, contract-test kit, and fixture report models.

A plugin must pass:

- Manifest validation
- API compatibility test
- Schema conformance tests
- Lifecycle tests, including forced timeout and reload rollback
- Permission-denial tests
- Resource-limit tests
- Determinism tests for transforms/renderers where applicable
- Security and dependency scan
- End-to-end test in a disposable host environment

Publish only signed builds produced by CI. Registry promotion should move through `draft → reviewed → verified → published`, rather than allowing arbitrary packages directly into production.

## Recommended implementation sequence

1. Build the registry, package verification, lockfile, manifest validator, and admin approval flow.
2. Implement the process/WASM supervisor, RPC gateway, permissions, quotas, and lifecycle manager.
3. Ship API v1 with `renderer`, `transform`, and `sink`; these cover the core reporting path cleanly.
4. Add source, UI, and command extension points only after the sandbox and capability gateway have proven stable.
5. Establish certification, observability, revocation, and incident runbooks before opening the registry to external teams.

The key architectural boundary is simple: plugins declare intent in a signed manifest; the host grants narrowly scoped capabilities; all work crosses a versioned RPC contract; and the host remains in control of lifecycle, retries, data access, and security."}}
