## 1. Architecture

```text
Reporting Core
 ├─ Plugin Registry
 │   ├─ Catalog / metadata store
 │   ├─ Artifact store
 │   ├─ Signature + policy verifier
 │   └─ Dependency resolver
 ├─ Plugin Manager
 │   ├─ Lifecycle coordinator
 │   ├─ Version/API compatibility checks
 │   ├─ Health supervision
 │   └─ Reload/rollback controller
 ├─ Plugin Runtime Boundary
 │   ├─ Sandbox worker / process / container
 │   ├─ Capability enforcement
 │   ├─ Resource limits
 │   └─ RPC transport
 ├─ Plugin API Gateway
 │   ├─ Versioned contracts
 │   ├─ Permission-scoped services
 │   ├─ Event delivery
 │   └─ Result validation
 └─ Reporting Domain
     ├─ Report generation pipeline
     ├─ Data/query services
     ├─ Renderers/exporters
     └─ Scheduling and delivery
```

The core never imports arbitrary plugin code into its own process. Plugins execute across a controlled boundary and receive only capability-scoped APIs.

## 2. Plugin model

A plugin is a signed, versioned bundle:

```text
plugin/
├─ manifest.json
├─ dist/
│  └─ entrypoint.js
├─ schemas/
│  └─ config.schema.json
├─ migrations/
├─ assets/
├─ signatures/
│  └─ manifest.sig
└─ README.md
```

Plugin types should be explicit rather than inferred:

| Type | Purpose | Typical extension points |
|---|---|---|
| `data-source` | Retrieve/report data | query provider, credential validation |
| `transform` | Modify normalized report data | pre-render transformation |
| `renderer` | Render report output | HTML, PDF, CSV, XLSX |
| `delivery` | Send completed reports | email, SFTP, webhook, archive |
| `template` | Provide report templates | template resolver, assets |
| `policy` | Enforce reporting rules | validation, redaction |
| `ui-extension` | Extend management UI | settings panels, preview widgets |
| `workflow` | Add reporting workflow steps | pre/post generation hooks |

A plugin can expose more than one extension only when declared in the manifest and approved by policy.

## 3. Registry

The registry is the source of truth for plugin availability, trust, compatibility, and rollout state.

### Registry records

```ts
interface PluginRecord {
  id: string;                     // e.g. "acme.salesforce-source"
  version: string;                // SemVer
  artifactDigest: string;         // sha256:...
  manifestDigest: string;
  publisher: PublisherIdentity;
  signatureStatus: "verified" | "rejected" | "unsigned-dev";
  status: PluginCatalogStatus;
  apiCompatibility: ApiCompatibility;
  publishedAt: string;
  deprecatedAt?: string;
  replacement?: PluginReference;
}

type PluginCatalogStatus =
  | "draft"
  | "published"
  | "deprecated"
  | "revoked"
  | "quarantined";
```

### Required registry capabilities

- Immutable artifacts: a released `id@version` can never be overwritten.
- Content-addressed storage: registry stores and verifies digests.
- Publisher trust: signing keys belong to organizations or teams, not individuals.
- Admission policy: validates manifests, signatures, dependency ranges, malware/static scans, license policy, and allowed capabilities.
- Revocation: supports immediate disabling of known-bad versions.
- Channels: `development`, `preview`, `stable`, and optionally tenant-private channels.
- Provenance: retain who published a version, build source revision, CI identity, SBOM, scan results, and approval record.
- Tenant targeting: enable a plugin version globally, for selected tenants, or for a staged percentage of jobs.

### Installation resolution

An installed plugin is a deployment-specific selection, separate from the catalog record:

```ts
interface PluginInstallation {
  installationId: string;
  tenantId?: string;
  plugin: { id: string; version: string; digest: string };
  enabled: boolean;
  configVersion: number;
  config: unknown;                // encrypted if sensitive
  grantedCapabilities: string[];
  rollout: "active" | "canary" | "disabled";
  desiredState: "running" | "stopped";
  resolvedDependencies: ResolvedPluginDependency[];
}
```

Use a lockfile-like resolution record per environment so a deployment can be reproduced exactly.

## 4. Manifest schema

Use JSON Schema Draft 2020-12. The schema itself is versioned independently from the platform API.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://reporting.example.com/schemas/plugin-manifest/v1.json",
  "type": "object",
  "required": [
    "manifestVersion",
    "id",
    "name",
    "version",
    "api",
    "runtime",
    "extensions",
    "permissions"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "manifestVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "const": "1.0"
