{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"# Reporting Plugin Platform — Reference Architecture

This design assumes plugins may be built by separate teams and process sensitive reporting data. Plugins are therefore isolated out-of-process and receive only explicitly granted capabilities.

```text
Plugin package → Registry → Admission / signature checks → Version resolver
                                                       ↓
Admin API → Plugin Manager → Runtime Supervisor → Isolated Plugin Worker
                                 │                         ↕
                                 │                    Capability RPC
                                 ↓                         ↕
                         Lifecycle + routing        Reporting Host Services
                                                   (data, secrets, storage,
                                                    rendering, export, logs)
```

## 1. Core components

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Plugin Registry | Stores immutable, signed plugin packages and metadata. |
| Admission Controller | Validates manifest, signatures, SBOM/vulnerabilities, policy, API compatibility, and permission requests. |
| Plugin Manager | Installs, enables, disables, upgrades, configures, and rolls back plugins. |
| Version Resolver | Selects an approved plugin version per tenant/environment and pins exact package digests. |
| Runtime Supervisor | Creates isolated workers, enforces CPU/memory/time/network limits, and performs lifecycle transitions. |
| Capability Broker | Exposes narrow host APIs through authenticated RPC; it is the only route from plugin code to host resources. |
| Schema Registry | Holds versioned schemas for plugin configuration, inputs, outputs, and report artifacts. |
| Audit/Observability Service | Captures lifecycle events, capability usage, failures, performance, and security decisions. |

The host owns reports, customer data, credentials, queues, storage, and delivery. Plugins never directly access these systems.

## 2. Plugin model

Support a small set of declared extension points:

- `data-source` — fetches normalized records from approved external systems.
- `transform` — validates, enriches, filters, or aggregates records.
- `visualization` — turns a report model into a chart/table/widget specification.
- `renderer` — produces a report format such as PDF, HTML, XLSX, or CSV.
- `destination` — sends an approved artifact to an email, storage, webhook, or business system.
- `policy` — makes bounded allow/deny/routing decisions; no arbitrary side effects.
- `scheduler` — proposes schedules; the host owns actual scheduling and execution.

A plugin declares exactly which extension points it implements. The host invokes typed operations for an extension point; it does not call arbitrary plugin methods.

## 3. Package and immutable identity

A package is a signed archive:

```text
report-plugin.tgz
├── plugin.json
├── dist/
│   └── entrypoint
├── schemas/
│   ├── config.schema.json
│   └── output.schema.json
├── migrations/
├── sbom.spdx.json
└── signatures/
```

An installed release is identified by all three values:

```text
pluginId + semanticVersion + sha256(package)
```

The registry must never overwrite a published version. Revocation, deprecation, and disabling are separate states.

Recommended plugin ID format: reverse-DNS lowercase, for example `com.acme.salesforce-source`.

## 4. Manifest schema

Use JSON Schema Draft 2020-12 for `plugin.json`. Reject unknown top-level fields so manifest changes are deliberate.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": [
    "manifestVersion", "id", "version", "name", "publisher",
    "api", "runtime", "extensions", "permissions", "entrypoint"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "manifestVersion": { "const": "1.0" },
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^[a-z0-9]+([.-][a-z0-9]+)+$"
    },
    "version": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(-[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?(\\+[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$"
    },
    "name": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 120 },
    "description": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 2000 },
    "publisher": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["id", "name"],
      "properties": {
        "id": { "type": "string" },
        "name": { "type": "string" },
        "supportUrl": { "type": "string", "format": "uri" }
      }
    },
    "api": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["hostApi"],
      "properties": {
        "hostApi": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^\\^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$" },
        "contractSchemas": {
          "type": "object",
          "additionalProperties": { "type": "string" }
        }
      }
    },
    "runtime": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["kind"],
      "properties": {
        "kind": { "enum": ["wasm-wasi", "node-worker", "python-worker", "container"] },
        "runtimeVersion": { "type": "string" },
        "maxConcurrency": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 100 },
        "startupTimeoutSeconds": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 120 },
        "invocationTimeoutSeconds": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 3600 }
      }
    },
    "extensions": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "required": ["type", "operation", "inputSchema", "outputSchema"],
        "properties": {
          "type": {
            "enum": ["data-source", "transform", "visualization", "renderer", "destination", "policy", "scheduler"]
          },
          "operation": { "type": "string" },
          "inputSchema": { "type": "string" },
          "outputSchema": { "type": "string" },
          "idempotency": { "enum": ["required", "supported", "none"] }
        }
      }
    },
    "permissions": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "required": ["capability"],
        "properties": {
          "capability": {
            "enum": [
              "report.read", "report.write",
              "artifact.read", "artifact.write",
              "plugin.kv.read", "plugin.kv.write",
              "secret.read", "network.egress",
              "destination.send", "log.write"
            ]
          },
          "scope": { "type": "object" },
          "reason": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 500 }
        }
      }
    },
    "configuration": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "schema": { "type": "string" },
        "secretFields": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } }
      }
    },
    "entrypoint": { "type": "string" },
    "state": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "schemaVersion": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1 },
        "migrationEntrypoint": { "type": "string" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Example permission request:

```json
{
  "capability": "network.egress",
  "scope": { "allow": ["https://api.example.com/v1/*"] },
  "reason": "Retrieve approved source records."
}
```

Production policy should reject wildcard outbound network access, plaintext secrets in configuration, and permissions irrelevant to declared extension points.

## 5. Registry and release workflow

A registry release moves through these states:

```text
draft → submitted → validated → approved → published
                         │
                         └→ rejected

published → deprecated | revoked
```

Admission checks must include:

- manifest and schema validation;
- package digest and publisher signature verification;
- publisher authorization for the plugin ID namespace;
- SBOM presence and vulnerability/license policy;
- host API compatibility;
- configuration and input/output schema validation;
- permission-policy approval;
- malware/dependency scanning;
- runtime image or dependency allowlist;
- reproducible-build attestation where practical.

Each environment has an allowlist of approved exact releases. A tenant configuration references the exact package digest, never merely `latest`.

## 6. Lifecycle contract

The host owns lifecycle state; plugins receive lifecycle RPC calls.

```text
discovered → validating → installed → disabled
                                      │
                              enable  ↓
                         resolving → starting → active
                                           │       │
                                           ↓       ↓
                                        failed   draining → stopped
                                                     │
                                                 unload/remove
```

### Required hooks

```ts
interface PluginLifecycle {
  onLoad(ctx: LoadContext): Promise<LoadResult>;
  onReady(ctx: ReadyContext): Promise<void>;
  onDrain(ctx: DrainContext): Promise<void>;
  onUnload(ctx: UnloadContext): Promise<void>;
  onHealthCheck(ctx: HealthContext): Promise<HealthStatus>;
}
```

Rules:

- `onLoad` may validate configuration and initialize ephemeral resources. It must be repeatable and side-effect-light.
- `onReady` runs only after the worker is eligible for traffic.
- `onDrain` stops new work and lets in-flight idempotent work finish before a host-set deadline.
- `onUnload` is best-effort cleanup only; the host must tolerate the process being killed without it.
- A lifecycle hook has a short, independent timeout. Failure transitions the release to `failed`; partial resources are removed by the supervisor.
- The host emits auditable lifecycle events with correlation IDs.

### Reload / upgrade

Reload is a staged replacement, never “replace code in a live process.”

1. Resolve and admit the target release.
2. Validate its configuration against its schema.
3. Start the new version in a separate worker.
4. Run `onLoad`, health checks, and contract smoke tests.
5. If state schema changes, run a declared migration against a versioned state snapshot.
6. Route new invocations to the new worker.
7. Drain the prior worker.
8. Stop it and retain rollback metadata until the release window expires.

If activation fails before routing changes, discard the new worker. If it fails afterward, route back only when the operation’s idempotency guarantees permit it; otherwise quarantine the affected jobs for explicit recovery.

## 7. Sandboxed execution boundary

Use a separate process or micro-VM/container per `pluginId + version` instance. Do not treat a language-level VM, such as JavaScript `vm`, as a security boundary.

Default sandbox policy:

- read-only package filesystem;
- no host filesystem mounts;
- no network;
- non-root, no privilege escalation, no shell tools;
- CPU, memory, process-count, file-descriptor, disk, and wall-clock limits;
- isolated temporary directory, deleted after execution;
- egress only through a policy-enforcing proxy;
- no inherited environment variables or credentials;
- structured RPC over local authenticated IPC;
- worker identity bound to plugin ID, version, tenant, and invocation.

WASM/WASI is the preferred runtime for untrusted transforms and renderers. Other runtimes should use hardened containers or micro-VMs. “In-process plugins” should be prohibited for third-party teams.

### Capability RPC

The worker uses a generated client, not direct database/HTTP/secret SDKs:

```ts
interface HostCapabilities {
  reports: {
    get(ref: ReportRef, fields: FieldMask): Promise<Report>;
    appendRecords(stream: AsyncIterable<RecordBatch>): Promise<WriteResult>;
  };
  artifacts: {
    create(meta: ArtifactMetadata): Promise<ArtifactWriter>;
    read(ref: ArtifactRef): Promise<ReadableStream>;
  };
  secrets: {
    lease(ref: SecretRef, purpose: string): Promise<ScopedSecretLease>;
  };
  storage: {
    get(key: string): Promise<Value | null>;
    compareAndSet(key: string, expectedVersion: string | null, value: Value): Promise<void>;
  };
  network: {
    fetch(request: ApprovedRequest): Promise<ApprovedResponse>;
  };
  logging: {
    emit(event: StructuredLogEvent): void;
  };
}
```

Every RPC request is authorized by capability, tenant, extension point, scope, and invocation ID. Secret leases are short-lived, redacted from logs, and non-exportable. Capability policy is evaluated by the host on every call, not trusted to the plugin.

## 8. Versioned API contract

Publish the host contract as a versioned SDK plus machine-readable schemas:

```text
host API: 2.3.0
plugin declares: ^2.1.0
negotiated API: 2.3.0
```

Use semantic versioning:

- **Major:** removed/changed behavior, incompatible schemas, altered security semantics.
- **Minor:** additive methods, optional fields, new extension capabilities.
- **Patch:** compatible fixes and clarifications only.

Compatibility rules:

- Hosts support the current major and previous major for a published support window.
- Plugins declare a semver range, but the registry records the resolved host API version at admission.
- Inputs add fields only as optional; consumers must ignore unknown fields.
- Outputs use explicit schema versions and discriminated types.
- Deprecated APIs remain documented, emit warnings, and have a published removal date.
- Host and SDK changes require compatibility tests against a maintained plugin conformance suite.

Every RPC envelope should carry:

```json
{
  "apiVersion": "2.3",
  "requestId": "uuid",
  "tenantId": "opaque-id",
  "plugin": { "id": "com.acme.salesforce-source", "version": "1.4.2" },
  "deadline": "2026-09-15T12:00:00Z",
  "traceContext": {}
}
```

Use stable, typed domain objects rather than exposing internal database models. Large inputs and artifacts should be streamed or passed by opaque handles, never copied through unbounded JSON payloads.

## 9. Data, state, and idempotency

Plugin state belongs to a namespace:

```text
tenant / plugin-id / major-plugin-version / state-key
```

The host provides encrypted, versioned KV storage with quotas and compare-and-set. Plugins cannot read another plugin’s state.

For side-effecting operations, the host supplies an idempotency key. Plugins must either:

- declare `idempotency: required` and persist/reuse completed outcomes; or
- declare `none`, in which case the host cannot automatically retry or fail over the operation.

Destinations should use an outbox record owned by the host so that report completion and delivery attempts are traceable and retry-safe.

## 10. Operational rules

Set platform-wide defaults, adjustable only downward by tenant policy:

| Limit | Recommended default |
|---|---:|
| Lifecycle hook timeout | 30 seconds |
| Standard invocation timeout | 5 minutes |
| Maximum invocation timeout | 60 minutes |
| Worker memory | 512 MiB |
| Concurrent invocations per worker | 10 |
| Plugin log event size | 16 KiB |
| Plugin state quota | 100 MiB |
| Consecutive failure quarantine | 5 failures / 10 minutes |

A circuit breaker should stop routing to unhealthy plugin versions, retain failed invocation evidence, and notify the plugin owner. Health checks must be cheap and must not call external systems by default.

Record immutable audit events for installs, approval decisions, permission grants, secret access, outbound calls, configuration changes, lifecycle transitions, and administrative actions.

## 11. Developer and governance experience

Provide five teams with:

- a typed SDK for each supported runtime;
- local runtime emulator with the same capability checks;
- manifest/schema validator;
- contract-test kit and golden fixtures;
- package/sign/publish CLI;
- sample plugins for every extension point;
- release dashboard showing adoption, failures, permission use, and deprecations;
- plugin ownership, escalation contact, and support-tier metadata.

Require CI gates before publishing:

```text
lint → unit tests → schema tests → contract tests → sandbox tests
     → SBOM/signature → package validation → registry admission
```

## 12. Recommended rollout

1. Build the registry, manifest validation, SDK contract, isolated worker supervisor, and read-only `transform` plugins first.
2. Add artifacts, rendering, and stateful plugins after observability and quotas are proven.
3. Add network and destination capabilities last, with explicit tenant approval and host-managed idempotency/outbox behavior.

This gives plugin teams a stable contract while preserving a hard boundary around reporting data, secrets, and side effects."}}
