{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"Adopt an **out-of-process, capability-based plugin platform**. Plugins never receive direct database, filesystem, network, or host-process access; they communicate with the reporting host through a versioned RPC contract and only receive explicitly granted capabilities.

This supports independently built plugins without allowing one plugin to destabilize reports, leak tenant data, or break upgrades.

## 1. Architecture

```text
Plugin bundle ──install/verify──> Plugin Registry
                                      │
                                      ├─ resolves compatible version
                                      ├─ checks policy and permissions
                                      └─ starts isolated Plugin Runtime
                                                    │
Reporting host <── versioned RPC ── Plugin API adapter
      │                                             │
      ├─ report lifecycle                         plugin hooks
      ├─ data/query service                       render/export/transform
      ├─ storage/secrets service                  plugin-owned state
      └─ observability service                    logs/metrics/traces
```

Core components:

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Plugin Registry | Installed versions, activation state, compatibility, dependencies, grants, trust status |
| Plugin Manager | Resolves plugins for a tenant/report, owns lifecycle and reload coordination |
| Plugin Runtime | Isolated process/container/WASI instance for one plugin version |
| API Gateway | Validates every request/response against the versioned API contract |
| Capability Broker | Enforces permissions, tenant scope, quotas, secrets, and data access |
| Extension Catalog | Lists registered report blocks, data sources, transforms, exporters, alerts, etc. |
| Plugin SDK | Typed client library, manifest helpers, local test runtime, contract-test fixtures |
| Audit/Telemetry Pipeline | Lifecycle audit log, health, usage, errors, API latency, resource limits |

Use one runtime instance per `{plugin id, version, tenant isolation scope}`. Do not run third-party plugin code in the reporting application process.

## 2. Plugin extension model

Make extension points explicit and limited. A plugin declares which it provides:

- `dataSource`: retrieves external data through approved outbound connections.
- `transform`: transforms a tabular/report dataset.
- `reportBlock`: supplies a configurable visual/report section.
- `exporter`: produces a report export format or delivery integration.
- `delivery`: sends scheduled reports to approved destinations.
- `alertRule`: evaluates report-derived conditions.
- `formatter`: defines constrained value formatting.
- `policy`: optional organization-owned policy extension; restricted to trusted plugins only.

Each extension is declarative in the manifest and has a separately versioned configuration schema. The host should not scan arbitrary code to discover functionality.

## 3. Package layout

```text
acme.salesforce-reports-2.4.1/
  manifest.json
  manifest.sig
  integrity.json
  runtime/
    plugin.wasm                 # preferred portable runtime
    # or server.js / server.exe for approved process runtime
  schemas/
    connection-config.schema.json
    report-block-config.schema.json
  migrations/
    1.0.0-to-2.0.0.json
  assets/
  README.md
  SBOM.spdx.json
```

Package requirements:

- Immutable package identity: `id + version + content digest`.
- Signed production bundles; registry verifies publisher certificate and signature.
- An SBOM and dependency/license scan are required for production installation.
- Plugin code may not modify its own bundle.
- Configuration and state live outside the bundle in host-managed storage.

## 4. Manifest schema

The canonical manifest is JSON Schema Draft 2020-12, stored and validated by the registry. Unknown top-level fields are rejected to prevent accidental unsupported behavior.

