## 1. Architecture

```text
Reporting Tool
 ├─ Plugin Registry
 │   ├─ discovery / install sources
 │   ├─ manifest validation
 │   ├─ signature + trust verification
 │   ├─ dependency resolution
 │   └─ enabled-version state
 ├─ Plugin Manager
 │   ├─ lifecycle orchestration
 │   ├─ hook dispatch
 │   ├─ health / crash handling
 │   └─ hot-reload coordination
 ├─ Plugin Host(s)
 │   ├─ one sandboxed worker/process per plugin instance
 │   ├─ permission enforcement
 │   ├─ versioned RPC bridge
 │   └─ resource quotas
 └─ Reporting Core
     ├─ stable domain events
     ├─ extension-point contracts
     └─ plugin-provided capabilities
```

Use one process or container per plugin instance, rather than an in-process module system. An in-process SDK can exist for trusted development plugins, but production plugins should use the same RPC contract and sandbox path.

## 2. Plugin model

A plugin declares one or more capabilities:

- `report.data-source` — supplies data to reports
- `report.transform` — transforms normalized report data
- `report.renderer` — renders an output format or visual component
- `report.exporter` — exports a completed report
- `report.delivery` — sends reports to approved destinations
- `report.policy` — contributes validation or compliance rules
- `report.ui-panel` — contributes UI through declarative UI definitions
- `report.command` — adds an operator/user command

Plugins must not arbitrarily patch core behavior. Every extension point has an explicit input/output schema, timeout, ordering policy, and failure policy.

## 3. Manifest

Ship a signed `report-plugin.json` with every package.

